<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · British Isles</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Connacht</h1><p class="st-meta">450 CE – 1474 CE (approx.) · capital Cruachan; later Tuam</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Western Irish province-kingdom whose O'Connor kings, Toirdelbach and Ruaidrí, became high kings before Anglo-Norman conquest reduced it.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/connachta">Connachta</a>, who gave the province its name, claimed descent from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conn-of-the-hundred-battles">Conn of the Hundred Battles</a>. By the 6th century their branches, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ui-briuin">Uí Briúin</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ui-fiachrach">Uí Fiachrach</a>, ruled the west, with kings inaugurated at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rathcroghan">Cruachan</a>, the great ritual site of Rathcroghan in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-roscommon">Roscommon</a>. The Uí Briúin gradually took control of the over-kingship.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Kings ruled through a network of subject kingdoms such as the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-ui-maine">Kingdom of Ui Maine</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ua-conchobair-dynasty">Ua Conchobair</a>, O'Connor, line rose in the 11th century; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toirdelbach-ua-conchobair">Toirdelbach Ua Conchobair</a>, king from 1106 to 1156, built bridges and castles, divided <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-munster">Munster</a> and claimed the high kingship. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ruaidri-ua-conchobair">Ruaidrí</a> became the last <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/high-king-of-ireland">High King of Ireland</a> and agreed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-windsor-1175">Treaty of Windsor</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-ii-of-england">Henry II</a> in 1175.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-normans">Anglo-Norman</a> lords led by the de Burghs received a grant of Connacht in 1227, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ua-conchobair-dynasty">O'Connors</a> were confined to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-roscommon">Roscommon</a>. Splits between rival O'Connor branches in the 14th century weakened the kingship further, and the title faded out in the later 15th century.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-connacht.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Connacht" width="320" height="418"><figcaption>Image: Erigena · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kingdom_of_Connacht-900.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>450 CE – 1474 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Cruachan; later Tuam</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Toirdelbach Ua Conchobair, Ruaidrí Ua Conchobair, Cathal Crobderg</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Irish, later Middle and Early Modern Irish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Ireland</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Connacht, Connaught</dd></dl></aside></div>

